#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379903 :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2y} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a circle.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is $y=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is $y=2-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ which is a parabola.\n\nSo, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380062 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380284 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0+0=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A$ is an element of $H$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $0,$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a matrix such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n0\\cdot k=0.\n$$\n\n This matrix belongs to $H.$ So, $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $H$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380291 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D,$ then  ${a}_{1}<0$ and ${a}_{2}<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n is a matrix from $D,$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}<0.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example,  if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=-1,$ then $A$ is an element of $D$ and $k\\in R,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* in  $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380338 :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380345 :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380352 :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380434 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function. \n\n![](images/80/80630fae7c4f1e29d855e9a68917156ccf3ecbefd0efc7bda5a9bd0e903c90db.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380455 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/e1/e1a02621cfacc791c1b100b01957195f1f41e9bf78b65286eabbd13363930c67.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $2$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=-1.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-2,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained in $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $[-2,2].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380615 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $⟨1,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381055 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{x{sin}^{2}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{z}$ is also defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{(1){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-3[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement I is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381355 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8457a8caa2f9dbdb627521e23029325f9edda757336a7a5fbbffbda14b4985.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2,$ and it occurs at the point $(-1,-2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values from $y=-2$ and greater, including the number $-2,$ so the range of the function is $[-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=-3.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381454 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For every natural $m$ there exists natural $n$ such that $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true since $5\\le n$ for some natural number $n$ (e.g., for $n=6$).\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis true for any natural $m.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=2.$ Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis true since we can take $n=3.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $n=m+1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀\\,m\\in N\\,(\\,{{∃n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{⏟}}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ And, according to statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381805 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a integer $y$ such that, for every integer $x,$ we have $x+y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $x+1=0$ is not true for some integer $x.$ Say, for $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(y):\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\n$$\n\nis false for every value of $y.$ For example,\n\n$$\nP(0):∀\\,x\\in Z,\\,x=0\n$$\n\nis false for any $x\\ne 0.$ In general, it's sufficient to take $x\\ne -y$ for any $y\\in Z.$ Therefore, its truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,y\\in Z,\\,∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,y\\in Z\\,(\\,{{∀x\\in Z,\\,x+y=0}_{⏟}}_{P(y)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(y)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381860 :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$x<1$\" is $(-\\infty ,1),$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$|x|\\ge 1$\" is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=(-\\infty ,1)\\cap ((-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ))=(-\\infty ,-1].\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is not empty, the statement $∃x\\in R,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382214 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1)=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,0)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(1,1)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-1}}}{2\\sqrt{1-1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II  only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382221 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x+y-z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,2)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,2)=\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-2}=\\sqrt{-1},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the number under the radical is negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 0+1-1}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382324 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function. \n\n![](images/1a/1a2b29a675770d1c8331672656a6054b052ebfebdf6bc61e5bd6459468087215.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph covers all $y$-values, so the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382351 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/47/47407d426cf9bb816c0b3dd052138b637bc73e7aa6d751278b20d8add020526d.png)\n\n \nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $8$ and the lowest $y$-value is $1.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $1$ and $8$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is indeed $[1,8].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in [0,3]$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[0,8],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[0,3]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $[0,3]$ onto $[0,8].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382400 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5dbc673c2c6a9bd0a4f06223133e21e2dcbbdbaa2dfe2de2b29d8824dd821e.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements II and III are true. In both cases, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382648 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382655 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(-1,2)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=-2x$ since $2=-2\\cdot (-1).$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨-1,2⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382722 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/3b/3b849edf2ae8976e0abd5e0aa43180ad61d90b01d6f229c2b7fc7898ea860400.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=(-1)+2=1$ and $f(1)=1+2=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382757 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/86/869be2ef6cb317b81c249b85b0afcbea23178dcf3bb77bb008e69375494439df.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}((-1)^{3}-(-1))=0$ and $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{3}-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(-1)=f(1)$ but $-1\\ne 1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,2],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383466 :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.45$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=2)=f(1,2)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.25\\cdot 0.6=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.1=0.25\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,1) & =0.3=0.75\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.45=0.75\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383561 :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(0,\\pi )$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383687 :question/worked-solution "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that we are given the set $∅.$ So, its power set must be ${∅}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${a,∅}.$ So, its power set must be ${∅,{a},{∅},{a,∅}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${∅}$ is ${∅,{∅}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385631 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}^{3}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385713 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/44/44a0d65bb3fc4f090dc82acc36a5c4657acaf5f9c8b0b80fe0a2cc30a3ac61e2.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $f(-3)=3-(-3)^{3}=30$ and $f(0)=3-{0}^{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385720 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a0/a0e70acd2c7844de100c5c0cb0f85480af17921308b23d505b7ea639ce4690b9.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}=0$ and $f(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(-1)=f(0)$ but $-1\\ne 0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,1],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385803 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}})}^{3} & ={(\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}})}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, choose $\\sqrt{2}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt{2}$ is $x=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$ Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\sqrt{8}}={({2}^{3/2})}^{1/3}={2}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393133 :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-2){y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396134 :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401586 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/bc/bc6b71d1fe69abd34dbd939dc62ede5201b75713e25456cf084e889bc44f449e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.4$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/4a/4a1fd7069abaa3b3fa4d9c286773d226798bf3ad565e85663cab44557fd635c1.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/26/260e8e959ca95a029a98bd4102106a66df418ea73c0640398c8fdebfdbf812f6.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402427 :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy=\\pm 1,$ which are hyperbolas.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402662 :question/worked-solution "For $x=0,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)={0}^{6}+1=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{(x-1)}^{3}=(0-1)^{3}=-1\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{6}+1)={0}^{6}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ \n\nHence, I is false, II is true and III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402929 :question/worked-solution "For $x=-1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-4.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\nf(-1)=4\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x).\n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nHence, I is true, and II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403564 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/1d/1d1c1cbe30e6f612f824adc76d712a98194ffccc5edefc11cccdaf07204513ba.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403647 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/21/215dd0c920e700a75896fc54751ec20d197487a2c992cd8910810c66129c14dc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/7e/7e1a0c5dd2b4aadf7efe78d903234b8d00cf4fa92eb7a3b41209a856f2d14b01.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/a9/a90d7306e3f88a31609e368f9362248d6a12b7922b6a9e8c9f980ab31e7fb140.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406841 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $Tx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406880 :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since if ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{2})+{x}_{3}(2{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{2}+(2{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{b}_{2})+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2}(2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406881 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406882 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-b)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-c)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883 :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406949 :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is false. The antecedent \"$12$ is divisible by $3$\" is true, and the consequent \"$9$ is divisible by $4$\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. The antecedent \"$5$ is divisible by $2$\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Again, the antecedent \"$9$ is divisible by $5$\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406950 :question/worked-solution "An implication $A⇒B$ is true *unless* $A$ is true and $B$ is false. In other words, the implication is true unless the \"promise\" is broken.\n\n- Statement I is true. The antecedent \"$7$ is an even number\" is false, so the promise does not apply. Regardless of the consequent, the promise is *not* broken, and the statement is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The antecedent \"$6$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$7$ is an even number\" is false. So the promise is broken, and the statement is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. The antecedent \"$6$ is an even number\" is true, and the consequent \"$7$ is an odd number\" is true. So the promise is kept, and the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406982 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & ⟹\\,x={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/89/899357795c6d5b81d3cb619d71493ad787645697fe1f2fcecda78c1b875f388e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407133 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exist rationals $q$ and $p,$ such that $p\\cdot q=-1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, $2p=-1$ for some rational number $p$ (e.g., for $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$).\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(q):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(q)$ is true for some $q\\in Q$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(0):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot 0=-1\n$$\n\nis false. However, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∃\\,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nis true, since we can take $p=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,q,p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃q\\in Q\\,(\\,{{∃p\\in Q,\\,p\\cdot q=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(q)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(q)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to the discussion in statement II, this is the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407498 :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407499 :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n8x+6y=2(4x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(8x+6y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407500 :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n8x+6y=2(4x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(8x+6y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $8(0)+6(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407505 :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=6(4x+5y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+5y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=3(8x+10y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $8x+10y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have $24(-1)+30(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407778 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{1}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{3}$ can not be a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}.$ As a result ${v}_{2}+{v}_{3}$ can't be written as combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$ too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407814 :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407886 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5f/5fd931a87d6a5fb32d10ed931f443a8175f0760eba9fe2fe69336f8f79e9fd68.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $f(2)=2(-2)+1=-3$ and $f(4)=2\\cdot 4+1=9.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[-2,4]$ that map to the same element in $[-3,9].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408038 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409434 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409435 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $9∤(17-2).$ Notice that $17-2=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $5∤(23-(-10)).$ Notice that $23-(-10)=33,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(33-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(33-12)÷7=21÷7=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409440 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $2∣(12-36).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(12-36)÷2=-24÷2=-12\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $4∤(-9-18).$ Notice that $-9-18=-27,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $8\\,|\\,(21-45).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(21-45)÷8=-24÷8=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409441 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $3∤(-5-11).$ Notice that $-5-11=-16,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $7\\,|\\,(-7-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-0)÷7=-7÷7=-7\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $9∤(6-25).$ Notice that $6-25=-19,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409482 :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}765 & = & 221\\cdot 3 & + & 102 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 221 & = & 102\\cdot 2 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 102 & = & 17\\cdot 6 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $17.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $765$ and $221$ is $17:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{gcd}(765,221)>1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409483 :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5719 & = & 2669\\cdot 2 & + & 381 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2669 & = & 381\\cdot 7 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 381 & = & 2\\cdot 190 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2 & = & 1\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $1.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $5719$ and $2669$ is $1:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1,$ the numbers are coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3},1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}_{y}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is also true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{1})=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409584 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409888 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q) & =1-{1}_{A}(q) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1,$ we conclude that $q$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409890 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(t),{1}_{B}(t)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1,$ we conclude that $t$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409891 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409893 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0) & =1-{1}_{A}(0) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1,$ we conclude that $0$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410006 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/3575afe4fd4e5aa6754496154986b272a43fb5c035ec8d69621d52fad16ee1ea.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2,$ and it occurs at the point $(0,2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values lower than $y=2$, including $y=2$ itself. So the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2]$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=3\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410009 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/c7/c79c1123036c978c8c4da514651c0bb1c8e3df34c8bbaff099bee601d568ed10.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph will cover all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410011 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3e074a2d029aeb3f4016332b9fb762fff6ec60ed7dce0290f97a95d6cb57c1.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, there is no highest or lowest $y$ value. The graph will cover any real value $y.$ So the range of the function is all $R..$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$ the function is a surjection. Therefore, $f$ is *not* an into function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410012 :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35bd21eef06d1ea63aba6e420d9842de61f9248c4fa26c2895f6825f4e7a3848.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values lower than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1)$,  there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $1\\notin (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is true, the function is a surjection.  So, $f$ is *not* a function of $R$ *into* $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411065 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨B.$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒B≡¬A∨B\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒B)$ is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411415 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411416 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∤$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∣$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤$, we have $2∤4,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $4∤6,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $2∣6,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411423 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411427 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411677 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x>y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $>$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $≯$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\le $) are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $6>8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $7>2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\le ,$ we have $9\\le 1,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411678 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $6∣3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $5∣8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $7∤9,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411679 :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ or equivalently, $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $6∣(x-y-4).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $6∣(0-2-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $6∣(1-9-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $6∤(4-13-4),$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411856 :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412421 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412961 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412977 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $7≡2\\,(\\text{mod }5),$ we have that $[7]_{5}=[2]_{5}.$ Therefore, $[7]_{5}\\cap [2]_{5}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9]_{4} & =[1+4\\cdot 2]_{4}=[1]_{4} \\\\ [15]_{4} & =[3+4\\cdot 3]_{4}=[3]_{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{4}\\cup [9]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [15]_{4} & =[0]_{4}\\cup [1]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [3]_{4} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412998 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413000 :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\ns\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $s$ to $0$ in $4$ steps, \nthe vector $s$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413394 :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x)=F(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ has period $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+2\\pi )=f(x+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413395 :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414417 :question/worked-solution "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414421 :question/worked-solution "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,  $S$ is a finite set and $|S|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $T=\\{2,3,4,5,6,7\\}$ is a finite set and $|T|=6.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $4\\ne 6,$ we conclude that $|S|\\ne |T|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415041 :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 5\\cdot a & ≡5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{5}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\n.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415043 :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(6,9)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415044 :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(3,4)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415045 :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10a & ≡10b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(5,3)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415046 :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10a & ≡10b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5) \\\\ 5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(5,2)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟺\\,9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415047 :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(2,8)=2.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415049 :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415051 :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(3,12)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415061 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{2}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}+{x}_{3}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{4}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415272 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415275 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=xy.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2},2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415281 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416481 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416485 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416486 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417703 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418155 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix,the matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $U$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418768 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1=-8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419635 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $5∤(-7-27).$ Notice that $-7-27=-34,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $6∤(-5-15).$ Notice that $-5-15=-20,$ which is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(11-18).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-18)÷7=-7÷7=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419636 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-5-8).$ Notice that $-5-8=-13,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $6∣(-23-(-5)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-23-(-5))÷6=-18÷6=-3\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $2\\,|\\,(-7-(-1)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-(-1))÷2=-6÷2=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419637 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-36-(-19)).$ Notice that $-36-(-19)=-17,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $10∤(15-(-26)).$ Notice that $15-(-26)=41,$ which is not divisible by $10.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(-27-9).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-27-9)÷4=-36÷4=-9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419686 :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420071 :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n14x+21y=7(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $7∣(14x+21y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420072 :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=2(3x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $3x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=3(2x-6y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x-6y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=2$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(2)-18(1)=-6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420258 :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $k=11.$ Then, $11∣11$ but $33∤11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $11∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$11∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $11∣k.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $3∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$3∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $3∣k.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420260 :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1.$ Therefore, we have $10\\,|\\,21b$ and $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1,$ which implies $10\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1.$ Therefore, we have $21\\,|\\,25b$ and $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1,$ which implies $21\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $b=5.$ Then, $15∣9\\cdot 5$ but $15∤5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420261 :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $b=19.$ Then, ${19}^{2}∣19\\cdot 19$ but ${19}^{2}∤19.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1.$ Therefore, we have $12\\,|\\,35b$ and $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1,$ which implies $12\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1.$ Therefore, we have $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1,$ which implies $22\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420262 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1$ and $2\\cdot 3=6.$ Therefore, we have $(2\\,|\\,a),(3\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1,$ which implies $(6\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=14.$ Then, $(7∣14)$ and $(14∣14),$ but $(98∤14).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1$ and ${2}^{3}\\cdot 3=24.$ Therefore, we have $({2}^{3}\\,|\\,c),(3\\,|\\,c),$ and $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1,$ which implies $(24\\,|\\,c).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
